    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm looking into some issues with a recent asphalt overlay we did here in Phoenix. We're seeing some premature longitudinal cracking in a specific section. Can you give me a technical summary of the primary causes for this kind of pavement failure, specifically looking at how thermal expansion and bitumen viscosity play into it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Longitudinal cracking in asphalt overlays, especially in a hot climate like Phoenix, is often a sign of underlying stresses, much of which is driven by temperature fluctuations. When the asphalt heats up, it expands, and when it cools, it contracts, creating internal stresses that, if not managed, lead to cracking. Bitumen viscosity plays a critical role here; if the binder is too stiff (high viscosity) at operating temperatures, it can't accommodate these thermal strains, leading to brittle failure. This is a classic case of the pavement being too rigid for the thermal movements, which is a failure mode you'd expect from a poorly designed or aged mix.",
      "source": "target",
