      "content": "My apologies, I was interrupted; I was about to say this situation is a perfect example of the difference between a well-designed pavement that can handle the thermal creep and one that is too brittle to accommodate the movement. If the subgrade is truly solid, we must look at localized, external factors that create stress concentrations, which is often where the real culprits hide. These localized issues can include differential settlement not visible in general compaction tests, localized moisture infiltration beneath the overlay, or even traffic loading patterns that are unusually heavy or concentrated in that specific section.\n\nAnother possibility, especially in Phoenix, is thermal gradients across the pavement width or length, where one side heats or cools much faster than the other, inducing shear stresses that the binder, if too stiff, cannot dissipate. Sometimes, the mixing process itself can leave subtle voids or areas of poor compaction within the overlay layer, acting as stress risers. These localized flaws are much more aggressive than uniform material issues.",
